# Toolchain and workspace that `emb flutter` / `emb cross` install into the
# tree. The documented workflow creates all three -- CI itself runs
# `emb flutter --flutter-version ...` and then `source ./setup_env.sh` -- so
# without these a developer who follows the README ends up with a 1.4 GB SDK
# and a 164 MB workspace staged one `git add -A` away from being committed.
/flutter/
/.config/
/setup_env.sh

# AOT snapshot that `emb bundle` drops beside an app (and outside its build/
# directory, so a per-app build/ ignore does not catch it). A build output, not
# source; no integration app commits one.
libapp.so*

# emb cross resolve state. Records the host tools a resolve ran with (rustc,
# Flutter and engine commits) and one entry per target the developer happens to
# have built, so it differs per machine and dirties the tree on every
# `emb cross`. Reproducibility for CI comes from pinning in the manifest and in
# CI's own environment rather than from a file every developer rewrites.
emb.lock

# Flutter build state for the integration fixtures: generated by
# `flutter pub get` / `flutter build bundle`, never source.
test/integration/*/.dart_tool/
examples/*/.dart_tool/
examples/*/build/
examples/*/pubspec.lock
packages/*/.dart_tool/
packages/*/pubspec.lock
packages/*/.packages
test/integration/*/build/
test/integration/*/pubspec.lock

# Python bytecode from the fixture drivers.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Working planning notes, which live outside the repository entirely and are
# deliberately never published: they carry phase labels, decision-record ids
# and section numbers that mean nothing to a reader who does not have the
# file, which is why commits, PR summaries and source comments say what they
# mean instead. A plan.md reached origin once by accident and had to be
# removed from history. Nothing should recreate one here, and this is the
# backstop for when something does.
plan.md
